/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/pcep.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("pcep", [{"t":"Fundamental terms: interpreting vs compiling, lexis, syntax and semantics, source code and the Python interpreter","hook":"It is your first week as a junior developer at Lantern Books, a small online bookshop. Priya, the team lead, forwards you a ticket: the nightly stock report script \"did nothing at all\" last night, not even the first line that announces it has started. A colleague insists Python must have crashed halfway through the run. You open the file and spot a missing closing bracket on the very last line. Could one typo at the bottom of a file really stop the first line from ever running, and how would you explain that to your colleague without sounding like you are guessing?","simple":"Computers only understand very low-level instructions made of ones and zeros. People write programs in friendlier languages like Python, so something has to translate. A compiler translates the whole program once, ahead of time, like translating an entire book before anyone reads it. An interpreter translates while the program runs, like a live interpreter at a meeting. Python works the second way. Every language also has rules: the allowed words (lexis), the grammar for putting words together (syntax), and whether the result makes sense (semantics). \"Dog the barked\" uses real words but bad grammar. \"The sofa barked\" has fine grammar but makes no sense. Python checks the grammar of the whole file before running anything, but only discovers nonsense when it reaches that line.","body":["A computer's processor only understands machine code: long sequences of binary instructions specific to that processor. People do not write that directly. Instead you write source code in a high-level programming language such as Python, and a program translates it into something the machine can execute. The PCEP (Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer) exam expects you to know the vocabulary for how that translation happens and for the rules any language imposes, because these words appear in questions long before you write a line of code.","There are two classic translation strategies. Compilation translates the whole program ahead of time into a separate executable file of machine code, which can then be run many times without the compiler. Interpretation reads the source code and executes it statement by statement each time the program runs, so the interpreter must always be present. Compiled programs tend to run faster and can be shipped without their source; interpreted programs are quicker to test and easier to move between platforms, because the same source runs anywhere an interpreter exists. Python is treated as an interpreted language: you run `python3 script.py` and the interpreter executes it. Internally, CPython first converts source into bytecode for its own virtual machine, but for the exam, Python is interpreted. The practical consequence is that a compiled program is tied to one kind of processor and operating system, while a Python script can be copied to Windows, macOS or Linux and run unchanged as long as an interpreter is installed there. The price is that every user needs that interpreter, and every run pays a small translation cost.","Every language, human or computer, has layers of rules. An alphabet is the set of symbols you may use. Lexis is the vocabulary: the set of valid words and symbols, such as keywords, operators, names and literals. Syntax is the grammar: the rules for how those words may be combined into valid statements. Semantics is meaning: whether a statement that is well formed actually makes sense. For example, `print(\"hi\"` is a syntax error because a parenthesis is missing, while `print(10 / 0)` is grammatically fine but fails when run because dividing by zero has no meaning. Think of the layers as a ladder: the text may use only legal symbols, those symbols must form legal words, the words must be arranged by the grammar, and only then can anyone ask whether the result means something. A problem on a lower rung stops you before the higher rungs are even checked.","The interpreter finds problems at different times, and the exam tests the difference. Before running anything, Python reads (parses) the whole file; a syntax error anywhere stops the program before even the first line executes, and the message shows the line with a caret under the problem. Run-time errors, called exceptions, appear only when the faulty line is actually reached, so earlier lines may already have produced output. Logic errors are the hardest: the program runs without complaint but gives the wrong answer, and no tool will flag them for you. You can tell the two apart from the screen. A syntax error message names the file and line, repeats the offending line with a caret, and ends with a line such as `SyntaxError: invalid syntax` (newer versions often print a more specific explanation). A run-time error prints a traceback that begins `Traceback (most recent call last):` and ends with the exception name and a message, for example `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero`.","CPython is the reference implementation of Python, written in the C language, and it is what you download from the official Python site. You can use it in two modes. Interactive mode, also called the REPL (read-eval-print loop), shows the `>>>` prompt and evaluates each line immediately, which is ideal for experiments. Script mode runs a saved `.py` file from start to finish. Source files are plain text, so any editor works, and IDLE (Integrated Development and Learning Environment), which ships with Python, gives you both an editor and a shell. In the REPL you do not even need `print()` to see a value: typing `2 + 3` at the `>>>` prompt echoes 5, which makes it a handy place to test an idea. A script, by contrast, shows nothing unless you print it.","```python\n# save as demo.py and run: python3 demo.py\nprint(\"start\")        # runs first\nprint(len(\"abc\"))     # 3\nprint(10 / 0)         # ZeroDivisionError here, at run time\nprint(\"never shown\")\n```","Consider a worked example. You save a file with three `print()` calls, but the third is missing its closing parenthesis. When you run it, nothing is printed at all: the interpreter reports a `SyntaxError` before executing anything, because parsing the whole file comes first. You fix the bracket, but now the second line is `print(1 / 0)`. This time the first line prints, then a `ZeroDivisionError` stops the program, so the third line never runs. Finally you fix that, but you wrote `area = width + height` where you meant multiplication; everything runs and prints a number, yet it is wrong. That is a logic error.","Common mistakes: thinking Python is compiled because it creates `.pyc` bytecode files; assuming a syntax error on line 10 lets lines 1 to 9 run first (it does not); and confusing lexis with syntax. A misspelled name such as `prnt` is lexically valid (it is a legal name), parses fine, and only fails at run time with `NameError`. Exam questions use clue words: \"translated once into an executable\" means compiler; \"executed line by line each time\" means interpreter; \"vocabulary\" means lexis; \"grammar\" or \"structure\" means syntax; \"meaning\" means semantics; \"found before execution\" means a syntax error."],"analogy":"Think of a play rehearsal. Before anyone acts, the director reads the whole script for missing pages and garbled lines; if a page is unreadable, rehearsal never starts. That is parsing and the syntax error. Once rehearsal begins, an actor may reach a scene that asks for something impossible, such as walking through a wall, and rehearsal stops right there, after the earlier scenes were already performed. That is a run-time error. The comparison stops working in one way: a real director might skip a bad page, but Python never skips a syntax error.","mnemonic":"Climb the ladder in order: Lexis, Syntax, Semantics means Words, Grammar, Meaning. Ask \"Is it a legal word? Is it put together correctly? Does it make sense?\" in that order.","terms":[["Source code","The human-readable program text you write, stored for Python in a plain-text .py file."],["Compiler","A program that translates the whole source code into machine code ahead of time, producing a separate executable."],["Interpreter","A program that reads and executes source code each time it runs; CPython is Python's reference interpreter."],["Lexis","The vocabulary of a language: the valid words and symbols it recognizes."],["Syntax","The grammar rules for combining lexical elements into valid statements."],["Semantics","The rules that decide whether a well-formed statement makes sense and what it means."],["REPL","The interactive read-eval-print loop, shown by the >>> prompt, that runs each line as you type it."]],"example":"You save a file with three print() calls, but the third is missing its closing parenthesis. When you run it, nothing is printed at all: the interpreter reports a SyntaxError before executing anything. Fix that, but make the second line print(1 / 0), and now the first line prints, then a ZeroDivisionError stops the program before the third line.","mistakes":[["Python is a compiled language because it creates .pyc files.","CPython does turn source into bytecode internally, but that bytecode still runs on Python's own virtual machine and needs the interpreter. For the PCEP exam, Python is an interpreted language."],["A syntax error on line 10 lets lines 1 to 9 run first.","Python parses the whole file before executing anything, so a syntax error anywhere means no line runs at all."],["Misspelling print as prnt is a syntax error.","prnt is a legal name and the line parses fine. It fails only at run time, with NameError, when that line is reached."],["A program that runs without any error message is correct.","Logic errors produce wrong results silently. Only comparing the output with what you expected reveals them."]],"tryit":[["Your script has five lines. Line 2 prints a greeting, line 4 divides a total by a count that turns out to be 0, and line 5 is missing a closing quote. When you run it, what appears on screen, and what happens after you fix only line 5?","On the first run nothing is printed except a SyntaxError for line 5, because Python parses the whole file before running anything. After fixing line 5, the greeting from line 2 prints, then line 4 raises ZeroDivisionError and the program stops, because run-time errors appear only when the faulty line is reached."]],"tip":"Exam questions often ask which kind of error a snippet produces. Missing brackets, bad indentation or misplaced keywords are syntax errors found before anything runs; valid code that does something impossible is a run-time error found only when that line is reached.","check":[["What is the key practical difference between compiling and interpreting?","A compiler translates the whole program once into an executable that runs without the compiler; an interpreter translates and runs the source every time, so the interpreter must be present."],["Is print(\"a\" + 5) a syntax error or a run-time error?","A run-time error (TypeError). The line is grammatically valid, but adding a string and an integer has no meaning, so it fails only when executed."],["Which is violated by writing prnt instead of print: lexis, syntax or semantics?","Not lexis or syntax: prnt is a valid name and the line parses. It fails at run time with NameError because the name is not defined, which is a semantic problem."],["A file has a syntax error on its last line. How many earlier lines run?","None. Python parses the whole file before executing it, so the SyntaxError is reported before any line runs."]]},{"t":"Python logic and structure: keywords, instructions, indentation and comments","hook":"You are pairing with Leo on the help desk team at Fernhill Community College. He has pasted a short script that should print one reminder for each student on a list and then a single summary line. Instead, the summary prints after every reminder, as if the loop swallowed it. The words in the file look perfect. You lean closer and notice the summary line starts four spaces in from the margin. Leo shrugs: \"Spaces cannot change what a program does, can they?\" In most languages he would be right. In Python, is he?","simple":"A Python program is a list of instructions read from top to bottom, usually one instruction per line. Some words, like if, for and while, are reserved: Python already uses them, so you cannot use them as names for your own things. Python also uses the spaces at the start of a line to show which lines belong together. Think of a recipe: \"If the oven is hot:\" followed by indented steps means those steps happen only when the oven is hot, while a line back at the left edge happens no matter what. Comments start with a # sign and are notes for people; Python skips them completely.","body":["A Python program is a sequence of instructions (statements) that the interpreter executes from top to bottom. Normally each statement sits on its own line, and the end of the line ends the statement; there is no semicolon at the end as in some other languages. You can put several short statements on one line separated by semicolons, and you can continue a long statement across lines inside parentheses, brackets or braces, or with a backslash at the end of a line, but the plain one-statement-per-line style is what you should write and what the exam usually shows. For example, a long sum written inside parentheses may continue onto the next line, because Python keeps reading until the bracket closes. Remember too that statements run strictly in order: a line cannot use a name that is only assigned further down the file.","Keywords are reserved words with a fixed meaning in the language, such as `if`, `else`, `elif`, `while`, `for`, `in`, `def`, `return`, `pass`, `break`, `continue`, `import`, `global`, `try`, `except`, `and`, `or`, `not`, `is`, `None`, `True` and `False`. You cannot use a keyword as a variable or function name: `for = 3` is a syntax error. Keywords are case sensitive: `True` is a keyword but `true` is an ordinary, and usually undefined, name. You can see the full list with `import keyword` followed by `print(keyword.kwlist)`. Each keyword has one job you will meet in later lessons: `if`, `elif` and `else` choose between paths, `while` and `for` repeat, `def` and `return` build functions, and `try` and `except` handle errors. Syntax highlighting in IDLE and most editors shows keywords in a distinct color, which is a quick visual check that you have not accidentally used one as a name.","Indentation is part of Python's syntax, not just style. A compound statement such as `if`, `while`, `for`, `def` or `try` ends its header line with a colon, and the lines that belong to it (its block, also called a suite) must be indented more than the header. The block ends when indentation returns to the earlier level. All lines in one block must use exactly the same indentation. PEP 8 (Python Enhancement Proposal 8, the official style guide) recommends four spaces per level. Mixing tabs and spaces inconsistently leads to `TabError`, and a wrong level leads to `IndentationError`; both are kinds of `SyntaxError`. Indentation replaces the curly braces that languages such as C or Java use to mark blocks, so in Python the visual layout and the program structure can never disagree. Blocks can nest: a `for` inside an `if` gets a second level of indentation, eight spaces in PEP 8 style, and each level closes when the code moves back out toward the left margin.","```python\nx = 7\nif x > 5:\n    print(\"big\")      # inside the if block\n    print(\"still in\") # same block, same indent\nprint(\"always runs\")  # back at the outer level\n# output: big / still in / always runs\n```","Comments start with `#` and run to the end of the line. The interpreter ignores them entirely, so they are for human readers: explain why the code does something, not what each obvious line does. A `#` inside a string literal is just a character, not a comment. Python has no special multi-line comment syntax; you put `#` on each line or, by convention, use a triple-quoted string, which is really a string literal that is evaluated and discarded. When a triple-quoted string is the first statement in a function or module it becomes its docstring, a piece of built-in documentation. Good comments explain intent, such as `# retry once because the sensor sometimes misses a reading`, while comments that merely restate the code, such as `# add 1 to x`, add clutter. Putting `#` in front of a line is also a quick way to switch it off during testing, a habit called commenting out.","Consider a worked example. A learner writes a loop that should print a running total. The header `for n in range(3):` is followed by two lines, the first indented four spaces and the second three. Python refuses to run the file and reports `IndentationError: unindent does not match any outer indentation level`. After fixing that, the learner moves `print(total)` back to the left margin; now it runs once after the loop instead of three times inside it. Both versions contain identical words, yet indentation alone changes whether the program runs and what it prints.","Common mistakes: forgetting the colon at the end of a header, which is a `SyntaxError`; indenting a line that has no header above it, which gives `IndentationError: unexpected indent`; ending a header with a colon and indenting nothing below it, which gives `IndentationError: expected an indented block` (the reason the `pass` keyword exists); writing `true` or `none` in lower case; and believing that a comment can serve as a block body. It cannot, because comments are discarded before the grammar is checked.","Exam questions in this area usually show a short snippet and ask whether it runs, what it prints, or which error appears. Clue words: \"reserved word\" or \"cannot be used as a name\" means keyword; \"belongs to the block\" means indentation level; \"ignored by the interpreter\" means comment; \"unexpected indent\" and \"expected an indented block\" name the two classic indentation errors. When a line's indentation is the only difference between answer choices, trace the columns carefully, because that is exactly what is being tested."],"analogy":"Indentation works like the outline of a school report. Sub-points indented under a heading belong to that heading, and when you return to the left margin you start a new heading. Python reads code the same way: the indented lines under a header that ends with a colon belong to it, and the first line back at the outer level is outside it. The comparison stops at strictness: a teacher might forgive one sub-point indented by three spaces instead of four, but Python demands that every line in a block line up exactly.","terms":[["Statement (instruction)","A single command the interpreter executes, normally one per line."],["Keyword","A reserved word with fixed meaning, such as if, for or def, that cannot be used as an identifier."],["Block (suite)","The group of indented statements belonging to a header line that ends with a colon."],["Comment","Text after # to the end of the line, ignored by the interpreter."],["IndentationError","The SyntaxError subclass raised when indentation is missing, unexpected or inconsistent."],["Docstring","A string literal placed first in a module or function, kept as its documentation."]],"example":"A learner writes a loop whose second body line is indented with three spaces instead of four. Python reports an IndentationError (unindent does not match any outer indentation level) before running anything. Re-indenting both lines to four spaces makes the program run, and moving the final print back to the margin makes it run once after the loop instead of on every pass.","mistakes":[["Indentation is only style, as in most other languages.","In Python indentation is syntax. It decides which statements belong to a block, and wrong or inconsistent indentation raises IndentationError before the program runs."],["true and none work because Python is forgiving about case.","Python is case sensitive. Only True, False and None are keywords; true is an ordinary name that raises NameError if it was never assigned."],["A comment can serve as an empty block body.","Comments are discarded before the grammar is checked, so a header followed only by a comment raises IndentationError: expected an indented block. Use pass instead."],["A # always starts a comment.","Inside a string literal, # is an ordinary character, so print(\"a # b\") prints a # b."]],"tryit":[["A teammate writes a function stub to fill in later: a def line ending in a colon, followed by a single line containing only # TODO, indented four spaces. The file refuses to run. What is wrong, and what is the smallest fix?","The comment is ignored, so the def header has no block at all, which raises IndentationError: expected an indented block. Adding an indented pass statement gives the header a real body that does nothing; the comment can stay."]],"tip":"True, False and None are capitalized keywords; lowercase true is just an undefined name. A # inside quotes is not a comment, and a comment alone can never be a block body.","check":[["What happens if you write if x > 3: and the next line is not indented?","Python raises IndentationError: expected an indented block, because a compound statement header must be followed by an indented block."],["Can you name a variable pass?","No. pass is a keyword, so pass = 1 is a SyntaxError."],["What does print(\"a # b\") output?","a # b. The # is part of the string literal, not the start of a comment."],["How many spaces per indentation level does PEP 8 recommend, and is it enforced?","Four spaces. The interpreter only requires consistency within a block; four spaces is the style convention."]]},{"t":"Literals: Boolean, integer, float, scientific notation and string literals","hook":"You are on the analytics team at Cedar Valley Water. Dana from billing sends a puzzled message: the monthly usage report says one customer used 2020 units, but the meter clearly reads 40. You open the script and find a line someone added while testing, `usage = \"20\" * 2`. Nobody typed a wrong digit; the 20 is right and so is the 2. So why did doubling twenty produce a four-digit number, and how could you have spotted the problem just by looking at how the value was written?","body":["A literal is a value written directly in your code, such as `42`, `3.5`, `\"cat\"` or `True`. The way you write it decides its type, which you can check with the built-in `type()` function. PCEP tests whether you can recognize each kind of literal on sight and predict its type, because type decides what operators do with the value. Literals are different from variables: `x` is a name that refers to a value, while `42` is the value itself. Knowing the type also tells you which operations are legal at all: you can divide one number by another, but dividing a string by a number is an error, so a quick glance at how a value is written prevents many bugs before they happen."],"locked":true},{"t":"Binary, octal and hexadecimal integer literals (0b, 0o, 0x)","hook":"You are the night-shift technician at Pinecrest Clinic, and the log from an infusion pump shows a fault code of `0x1F`. The pump's manual lists fault numbers in plain decimal, from 1 to 40. Your colleague Sam suggests the log is corrupted, because \"0x1F is not even a number.\" Before you phone the vendor's support line at 3 a.m., you want to be certain. Is `0x1F` a real number, and which entry in the manual does it point to?","body":["People normally write numbers in decimal (base 10), but computers store them in binary (base 2), and programmers often use octal (base 8) and hexadecimal (base 16) as compact ways to write binary patterns. Python lets you write integer literals in any of these bases by adding a prefix. The prefix only changes how you write the number; the result is always an ordinary `int`, and `print()` shows it in decimal. Why bother? Because some values are naturally bit patterns: a color code, a permission setting or a set of flags reads far more clearly as `0xFF` or `0b1010` than as 255 or 10."],"locked":true},{"t":"Variables and naming rules, reserved keywords and PEP 8 naming conventions","hook":"You have just joined the small development team at Riverside Bakery Supply, and Omar asks you to review a script before it goes live. One line reads `2nd_order = 15`, another uses `order-total`, and a third names a running total `sum`, which works fine until a later line calls `sum()` on a list and crashes. Omar shrugs: \"They are all just names. What is the difference?\" The review meeting starts in ten minutes. Which of these names will Python refuse outright, which will run but cause trouble later, and which merely break the team's style guide?","body":["A variable is a name that refers to a value stored in memory. In Python you create a variable simply by assigning to it: `age = 30`. There is no separate declaration and no fixed type attached to the name; the value carries the type, and the same name can later refer to a value of a different type, so `age = \"thirty\"` is legal. Python is therefore called dynamically typed. Using a variable before anything has been assigned to it raises `NameError`. Behind the scenes, a name is a label attached to an object: after `a = 10` and `b = a`, both names refer to the same int object. Assigning `b = 20` later moves only the `b` label, and `a` still refers to 10."],"locked":true},{"t":"Numeric operators: ** * / % // + - and the difference between / and //","hook":"You are covering the support inbox at Maplewood Tutoring, where a scheduling script turns each 125-minute session into hours and minutes for the timetable. A tutor named Ines writes in: her timetable says her session lasts 2.0833333333333335 hours. A second message reports that a refund calculation for a negative balance came out one dollar further from zero than expected. Both bugs trace back to the division operators in the code. Which operator should the script have used each time, and why does Python round negative numbers the way it does?","body":["Python's arithmetic operators are `+` (addition), `-` (subtraction), `*` (multiplication), `/` (true division), `//` (floor division), `%` (remainder, called modulo) and `**` (exponentiation). The PCEP exam loves these, especially the three division-related ones, so you need to predict both the value and the type of every result. Getting the type right is half the answer, because `3` and `3.0` print differently. These operators are the building blocks of almost every calculation a program makes, from totaling a shopping cart to working out whether a number is even, so fluency with them pays off in every later domain."],"locked":true},{"t":"String operators (+ and *), assignment and compound assignment operators (+=, *=, etc.)","hook":"You maintain the receipt script for Bluebird Cafe. Just before the lunch rush, the owner, Rosa, calls: the line that should print \"Table 4\" at the top of each receipt now crashes with a TypeError, and a test line meant to show four times four printed 4444 instead of 16. Both problems appeared after yesterday's quick edits, and both involve the familiar symbols + and *, which behave very differently once text is involved. What exactly do those symbols do to strings, and how do you fix both lines before the first order prints?","body":["Two arithmetic symbols also work on strings, with different meanings. The `+` operator concatenates (joins) two strings: `\"snow\" + \"ball\"` gives `\"snowball\"`. The `*` operator replicates a string a whole number of times: `\"ab\" * 3` gives `\"ababab\"`. The order of the operands for `*` does not matter, so `3 * \"ab\"` is the same. Multiplying by zero or a negative number gives an empty string `\"\"`. Neither operator adds a space for you: `\"Hello\" + \"world\"` is `\"Helloworld\"`. Both operators always build a brand-new string; they never change the strings you started with."],"locked":true},{"t":"Operator priority and binding, including right-to-left ** and unary minus","hook":"You are reviewing code at Northgate Robotics, where a junior engineer, Kai, wrote `penalty = -speed ** 2` to compute a cost that should always be positive. In testing, the route planner keeps choosing the fastest and riskiest path, because the penalty comes out negative and so looks like a reward. Kai insists that \"minus speed, squared\" must be positive. The expression has no parentheses, so Python had to decide on its own which operator to apply first. Which one did it choose, and why?","body":["When an expression contains several operators, Python uses priority (also called precedence) to decide which operations happen first, and binding (associativity) to decide the order among operators of the same priority. Parentheses override both, so in your own code, add them whenever the order is not obvious. The exam, however, gives you expressions without helpful parentheses and asks for the result, so you need the table in your head. Python's rules match school arithmetic for the familiar operators, so multiplication still comes before addition, but Python has many more operators, and two of its rules surprise people who learned math on paper."],"locked":true},{"t":"Bitwise operators: ~ & ^ | << >>","hook":"You are the on-call engineer for the door access system at Greenleaf Library. At 2 a.m. an alert fires: a visitor badge just opened the staff room. Badge permissions are stored as one integer, with each bit standing for one door, and a nightly script is supposed to remove staff room access from visitor badges. Last week a colleague changed that line from `perms &= ~4` to `perms ^= 4`, reasoning that both \"turn the bit off.\" The badge had been fine for months. How could one changed operator, acting on bits you cannot even see in the decimal value, unlock a door?","body":["Bitwise operators work on the individual binary digits (bits) of integers rather than on their overall values. They only accept integers, including Booleans, which are integers; using them on floats raises `TypeError`. You will meet them in low-level work such as flags, permissions and network masks, and the exam checks that you can compute them by hand. Think of each number as a row of switches, and each operator as a rule for combining the switches column by column. Inside the computer every int is already stored as bits, so these operators are fast and map directly onto what the hardware does."],"locked":true},{"t":"Boolean and relational operators, float accuracy and rounding surprises","hook":"You support the checkout system at Brightwater Hardware. A cashier, Tom, reports that the exact-change discount never triggers, even when a customer buys two items priced 0.10 and 0.20 and the screen clearly shows 0.30. You open the code and find `if total == 0.3:`, which looks flawless. Another note on your desk says a price of 2.5 was rounded to 2 on a receipt, not 3. Is the computer bad at arithmetic, or is something about how decimal numbers live in memory catching everyone out?","body":["Relational (comparison) operators compare two values and return a Boolean: `==` equal, `!=` not equal, `<` less than, `>` greater than, `<=` less than or equal, and `>=` greater than or equal. Note that `=` assigns while `==` compares. Numbers of different types compare by value, so `1 == 1.0` is `True` and `True == 1` is also `True`. Strings compare character by character using their character codes, so `\"B\" < \"a\"` is `True` because uppercase letters come before lowercase ones, and `\"apple\" < \"apricot\"` is `True` because `p` comes before `r` at the first difference. Comparing a string with a number using `<` raises `TypeError`, although `==` simply returns `False`. Every comparison produces a `bool`, so you can store it, print it or combine it with other comparisons: `is_adult = age >= 18` is a perfectly ordinary statement."],"locked":true},{"t":"Type casting with int(), float(), str() and bool()","hook":"You are helping Grace, the volunteer coordinator at Oakridge Food Pantry, with a sign-up script. Volunteers type their age and whether they want weekend shifts, and the script decides who may lift heavy boxes. Yesterday the script crashed when someone typed their age as 17.5. Today every volunteer is marked as wanting weekend shifts, including the ones who typed no. Grace suspects the computer is ignoring people. Both bugs share one root cause: values in the wrong type, converted the wrong way. Which conversion function fixes each one, and which function is the trap?","body":["Type casting (type conversion) means creating a value of one type from a value of another. Python does some conversions automatically, for example turning an int into a float when you add `2 + 0.5`, but it never silently turns a string into a number or a number into a string. For those you call a conversion function: `int()`, `float()`, `str()` or `bool()`. Each returns a new value; the original is unchanged, so `x = \"5\"` followed by `int(x)` on its own line leaves `x` a string unless you assign the result, as in `x = int(x)`. You need casting most often at the edges of a program, where data arrives as text from a keyboard or a file and has to become a number before you can calculate with it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Console I/O: print() with sep= and end=, input() returning a string, converting input to numbers","hook":"You are the only programmer at Sunfield Animal Shelter, and the front desk logs donations with a small console script. Mia at the desk sends you a photo of the screen: a donor gave 20 and then 5, and the running total reads 205. In the same photo, the thank-you message sits on its own line below the amount instead of beside it. Nothing crashed, and all the data is there. What is the script doing with the text it reads and writes, and which small arguments control where each character lands?","body":["Console programs talk to the user through two built-in functions: `print()` for output and `input()` for input. Both show up constantly in PCEP questions, usually as \"what exactly is printed?\", so small details such as spaces and line breaks matter. The console, also called the terminal or standard output, is simply the text window where your script runs. Almost every exam snippet ends in a `print()` call, so reading output exactly, character by character, is a skill you will use in every domain, not only this one."],"locked":true},{"t":"If, if-else and if-elif-else statements, and why the order of elif conditions matters","hook":"You have just joined the small developer team at Marlow Lane Freight, and the first ticket in your queue is angry. A customer shipped a 30 kg crate of machine parts and was charged the parcel rate, a fraction of the freight price. Accounting wants to know how many other crates slipped through. You open the pricing script and find four tidy lines: an `if`, two `elif` branches and an `else`. Nothing crashes, nothing logs an error, and every test the previous developer wrote still passes. Yet somewhere in those few lines, a heavy crate is being treated like a small box. How can code that looks perfectly reasonable send the wrong answer every single time?","body":["Conditional statements let a program choose what to do based on data. Without them, every program would do exactly the same thing every time, whatever its input. The simplest form is `if`: a header line made of the keyword, a condition and a colon, followed by an indented block. If the condition is true, the block runs; if not, Python skips the whole block and carries on with the first statement after it. A typical header looks like `if temperature > 30:`, and the indented lines below it, conventionally four spaces in, form the body that depends on that test."],"locked":true},{"t":"Multiple conditions with and, or and not; truthy and falsy values","hook":"It is Monday morning at Fernhill Community Library, and Jonah, the volunteer who maintains the member portal, has a worried message for you. Over the weekend, ordinary members were able to open the staff page that edits book records. Nobody was given new permissions. The only change was one line he added on Friday to let editors in as well as admins: `if role == \"admin\" or \"editor\":`. It reads like plain English, and it ran without a single error. Yet it seems to have opened the door for everyone. How can a condition that sounds exactly right be true for every person who logs in?","body":["Real decisions often depend on more than one fact, so Python lets you combine conditions with the logical operators `and`, `or` and `not`. The expression `a and b` is true only when both are true. The expression `a or b` is true when at least one is true. The expression `not a` flips a value. For example, `if age >= 18 and has_ticket:` requires both facts, while `if day == \"Sat\" or day == \"Sun\":` accepts either one. These are keywords, written in lower case; the symbols `&&`, `||` and `!` from other languages are not valid Python and produce a `SyntaxError`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Nested conditional statements and indentation","hook":"It is a Monday morning in January, and Rosa, the facilities manager at Brightwater Primary School, is on the phone. The heating contractor has been reading the boiler log and wants to know why, every night after the heating switches off, your thermostat script keeps sending the command to hold the temperature. The script ran without a single error all week. You open the file and look: an `if heating_on:` with a smaller `if` and `else` tucked inside. Every word is exactly what you meant. Then you notice that the `else` sits four spaces further left than you remember, lined up with the outer `if` instead of the inner one. Can four spaces really change what a program does?","body":["A nested conditional is an `if` statement placed inside the block of another `if`, `elif` or `else`. You use nesting when a second decision only makes sense after the first one has been made: first check that a user is logged in, then, only for logged-in users, check whether they are an administrator. The inner decision is not even considered unless the program has entered the outer block. If the outer condition is false, Python skips the whole outer block, inner `if` included, without evaluating anything inside it."],"locked":true},{"t":"The pass instruction as a placeholder body","hook":"You are sketching a to-do app for the volunteers at Cedar Hollow Food Bank, and you want to try the menu before writing any of the real features. You type `def add_task():`, press Enter, add a comment that says to finish this later, and do the same for `remove_task` and `list_tasks`. You run the file to see your menu, and Python refuses to start: `IndentationError: expected an indented block`. There is nothing wrong with the menu, and the comments clearly say what each function will do. So why will Python not run a file whose only problem is that some parts are not written yet?","body":["Python requires every compound statement header, meaning a line ending in a colon such as `if`, `elif`, `else`, `while`, `for`, `def`, `class`, `try` or `except`, to be followed by at least one indented statement. Sometimes you have nothing to put there yet, or you deliberately want nothing to happen. Leaving the block empty is a syntax error, reported as `IndentationError: expected an indented block`, and because it is detected before the program starts, not one line of the file runs. A comment does not help, because the interpreter discards comments when it reads the code, and the block is still empty."],"locked":true},{"t":"While loops, loop conditions and avoiding infinite loops","hook":"At 7 a.m. Theo, who runs the small savings app for Willow Creek Credit Union members, finds the overnight report job still running. It normally finishes in seconds. The server fan is roaring, the log has stopped growing, and the job has been stuck on one member's projection for six hours. That member's savings plan says the monthly deposit is 0, because they paused contributions last week. The script that projects how many months until they reach their goal is a simple `while` loop that has worked for years. Why would a single zero turn a three-line loop into a program that never stops?","body":["A `while` loop repeats a block as long as its condition is true. Python checks the condition before each pass through the body, and each pass is called an iteration. If the condition is true, the body runs, then Python goes back to the header and checks again. When the condition is false, the loop ends and execution continues with the first statement after the block. If the condition is false the very first time, the body never runs at all, so a `while` loop may run zero times. That makes it different from loops in some other languages that always run at least once."],"locked":true},{"t":"For loops over range() with start, stop and step, including negative steps and empty ranges","hook":"The science fair at Juniper Ridge High School opens in ten minutes, and Amara's rocket display is supposed to count down from 10 on the big screen before the model launches. She tests it one last time. The screen stays blank, then shows the word Go. No numbers, no error, nothing in red. She had changed one thing that morning, tidying `range(10, 0, -1)` to the shorter `range(10, 0)` because the extra number looked unnecessary. The countdown worked yesterday. Now it silently skips every number. What did that small missing value really do, and why did Python not complain?","body":["A `for` loop runs its body once for each item in a sequence. To repeat something a known number of times, you usually loop over `range()`, which produces a series of integers on demand without building a list in memory. The loop variable takes each value in turn, and you can use it inside the body. The header always has the shape `for name in range(...):`, followed by an indented block, and the loop ends by itself when the range has no more values."],"locked":true},{"t":"Iterating over strings, lists and other sequences with for","hook":"The sign-up page for the Pinecrest Running Club has a new password checker, and Malik, the volunteer who wrote it, is puzzled. Members keep complaining that the page says their password has only one digit when it clearly has three. Malik's code loops over every character, checks `ch.isdigit()`, and adds one to a counter. He has stared at the loop for an hour, and every individual line looks right. The bug is not in what the loop does with each character. It is in where one innocent line, `digits = 0`, happens to sit. How can a single misplaced line make a loop forget almost everything it has counted?","body":["A `for` loop is not limited to numbers. It works with any iterable, meaning any object that can hand out its items one at a time. Strings, lists, tuples, dictionaries, sets and ranges are all iterable. The syntax is always `for name in iterable:`, and on each pass the name is bound to the next item. The loop ends by itself when the items run out, so you never need a counter or an end condition. Looping over an empty sequence runs the body zero times, without any error."],"locked":true},{"t":"Break and continue, and how break affects only the innermost loop","hook":"Opening night at the Riverside Playhouse is sold out except for a handful of seats, and Nadia at the box office is using the seat finder you wrote to place a late arrival. She asks for the first free seat, nearest the stage. The program proudly answers row K, near the back, although she can see an empty seat in row C on her chart. Your code clearly says `break` the moment it finds a free seat. Somehow it kept searching anyway, row after row, and reported the last free seat instead of the first. If `break` means stop, why did your loop keep going?","body":["Normally a loop runs its whole body on every iteration and stops only when its condition fails or its sequence runs out. Two keywords let you change that from inside the body. `break` ends the loop immediately; execution jumps to the first statement after the loop. `continue` ends only the current iteration; the rest of the body is skipped and the loop moves on to its next iteration. For a `while` loop that means re-checking the condition; for a `for` loop it means taking the next item. Neither keyword takes an argument."],"locked":true},{"t":"While-else and for-else: when the else clause runs and when break skips it","hook":"The help desk at Oakmere College is getting odd calls. Students who mistype their password once see the message Account locked, then type the right password and get in anyway. Elena, the student worker who maintains the login script, is baffled. The code gives three attempts with a `for` loop, breaks out on a correct password, and has an `else` that prints the lock message. She tested it with three wrong attempts and it locked correctly. The words are all right. The only thing she cannot explain is why the lock message appears after a single mistake. Which statement does that `else` actually belong to?","body":["Python lets a loop have an `else` clause, which surprises people who know `else` only from `if`. The `else` block is written at the same indentation as the `for` or `while` header and runs once, after the loop, if the loop finished normally. Normally means the `while` condition became false or the `for` loop ran out of items. If the loop was ended by `break`, the `else` block is skipped. Either way, execution then continues with whatever statement follows the `else` block, which runs in both cases."],"locked":true},{"t":"Nested loops and counting iterations","hook":"The Starlight Cinema in Ashford Falls is reopening after renovation, and Leon, the manager, wants seat labels printed for every seat before Friday. Your script prints labels from A1 to E10 using one loop inside another. Leon has ordered exactly 50 blank label cards. On Thursday he tells you the last two seats in rows D and E have been removed for a wheelchair space, and asks how many cards he can send back. You add one line with a `break` to skip the closed seats. Now you need an exact number, not a guess, before the print shop closes. How many labels will your loops really print?","body":["A nested loop is a loop inside the body of another loop. For every single iteration of the outer loop, the inner loop runs from start to finish. Nested loops are how you work with two-dimensional data such as rows and columns, generate every pair of items, or print patterns and tables. The outer loop decides how many times the inner loop is started; the inner loop decides how much work each start does. Both loops can be `for` loops, both can be `while` loops, or you can mix them."],"locked":true},{"t":"The value of the loop variable after a for loop ends","hook":"Sam runs the parts inventory for Copperfield Bike Repair, and a customer is standing at the counter waiting for a brake cable. Sam types the part code into the lookup script you wrote, and it confidently reports the item at the end of the list, a set of handlebar grips. The brake cable is not in stock at all, but the program did not say so. Your search loop breaks when it finds a match and then prints the item at the loop's index. It worked every time you tested it with parts that existed. What value does that index hold when the loop finds nothing and simply runs out of items?","body":["In Python, the loop variable of a `for` loop is an ordinary variable in the surrounding scope. It does not disappear when the loop ends. After the loop, it still holds the last value it was assigned. Many other languages treat loop variables as local to the loop, so this is a favorite PCEP trap, and questions often print the variable right after the loop. Nothing resets or deletes it for you."],"locked":true},{"t":"Lists: building, indexing (including negative indexes) and slicing","hook":"Priya keeps the weather station for Hollow Pine Middle School's science club, and every evening a short script posts today's temperature to the club noticeboard. Tonight the script crashes with `IndexError: list index out of range`. The list holds exactly seven readings, one for each day of the week, and the line that fails asks for item 7, which seemed like the obvious place to find the seventh day. Strangely, a different line asking for items 5 to 10 ran perfectly, even though there is nothing at position 10. Why does one request crash while another, which reaches even further, quietly works?","body":["A list is an ordered, changeable (mutable) collection of values. You write it with square brackets and commas: `nums = [10, 20, 30]`. A list may contain values of mixed types, duplicates and even other lists, so `[1, \"two\", 3.0, [4]]` is perfectly legal. An empty list is `[]` or `list()`. You can also build a list from any iterable, so `list(\"abc\")` is `['a', 'b', 'c']` and `list(range(3))` is `[0, 1, 2]`. Two operators build new lists from old ones: `+` concatenates (`[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`) and `*` repeats (`[0] * 3` is `[0, 0, 0]`). Lists matter because almost every real program handles a collection of things: scores, names, readings or lines of a file."],"locked":true},{"t":"List methods and functions: append(), insert(), index(), remove(), sort(), len(), sorted(), del","hook":"It is Friday afternoon at Maple Ridge Middle School, and Priya, the only person on staff who knows any Python, gets a message from the vice principal: the honor roll script printed \"None\" instead of a ranked list of names, and the assembly is Monday morning. You open the file and find a single line, `ranked = names.sort()`, followed by `print(ranked)`. The list of names is right there, perfectly sorted, yet the variable meant to hold it is empty. Nothing crashed and no error appeared. So where did the sorted list go, and why did Python hand back nothing at all?","body":["Lists come with methods, which you call with dot notation on the list itself (`lst.append(4)`), and they also work with general built-in functions such as `len()` and `sorted()` and with the `del` statement. The difference matters on the exam: methods like `append()` and `sort()` change the list in place and return `None`, while functions like `sorted()` leave the list alone and return something new. Knowing which tool mutates and which returns a value is the key to predicting output."],"locked":true},{"t":"Iterating through lists, in and not in, list comprehensions with conditions","hook":"Daniel runs the volunteer sign-up desk for the Riverbend Food Bank, and the spreadsheet export he gets every Monday is a long Python list of shift hours. His manager wants two things by noon: a list of only the shifts longer than four hours, and a label of \"long\" or \"short\" for every shift so the chart lines up with the original rows. He writes two list comprehensions that look almost identical, yet one returns 12 items and the other returns 31. The chart is broken, the noon deadline is close, and he cannot see why two nearly identical lines behave so differently. What is the one placement detail he is missing?","body":["The most common thing to do with a list is to process each item in turn. A `for` loop over the list gives you each element directly, one per iteration: `for price in prices: total += price`. The loop variable is simply bound to the next element each time; it is not a position. If you also need positions, loop over `range(len(prices))` or use `enumerate(prices)`, which yields pairs of index and value. When you need to change items in place, use the index form (`prices[i] = prices[i] * 2`), because assigning to the loop variable only rebinds that name and does not modify the list."],"locked":true},{"t":"Copying vs aliasing lists: b = a compared with a[:] or list(a)","hook":"Sam is building a seating tool for the Lakeview Community Theater. Each new show should start with a copy of the default seating list, and staff then mark seats as sold. On opening night, the box office calls: the Thursday matinee shows seats as sold that nobody bought, and the Saturday show has the same \"sold\" seats. You open the code and see `show_seats = default_seats`, followed by changes to `show_seats`. Every show has been writing into the very same list all week. How can a single equals sign tie three shows together, and what one change would give each show its own list?","body":["In Python, a variable does not contain a list; it holds a reference to a list object stored somewhere in memory. Think of the name as a label tied to the object rather than a box holding it. This small fact explains one of the most tested behaviors on the PCEP (Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer) exam. When you write `b = a` where `a` is a list, Python copies nothing. It ties a second label, `b`, to the very same list object. Now `a` and `b` are two names (aliases) for one list, and a change made through either name is visible through the other."],"locked":true},{"t":"Nested lists and matrices (list of lists)","hook":"Leo is writing a simple booking tool for the Harborview Bowling Center, with 4 lanes and 6 time slots stored as a grid of zeros. He tests his first booking, lane 2 at the 3 o'clock slot, prints the grid, and freezes: every lane now shows that same slot as booked. Customers will be turned away for slots that are actually free. The code that changed the grid is one short line, and it looks correct. The real problem is hidden in the line that built the grid in the first place. How can a grid of independent rows end up acting like four copies of one row?","body":["A list can contain other lists. A list whose items are all lists of the same length is a convenient way to represent a matrix or grid: a table with rows and columns, such as a game board, a seating plan, a timetable or a small spreadsheet of numbers. Each inner list is one row, and the position within that row is the column. Python has no separate built-in matrix type at PCEP level, so a list of lists is the standard tool, and the exam expects you to index, loop over and build them confidently."],"locked":true},{"t":"Tuples: building (including one-item tuples), indexing, slicing, immutability and tuples vs lists","hook":"Ava maintains the inventory script for Pinecrest Hardware, and a weekend change has made one report crash with \"TypeError: 'int' object is not subscriptable\". The line she added looks harmless: `sizes = (12)`, followed later by `sizes[0]`. Monday's delivery truck is waiting for that report. She is sure she wrote a tuple, because the parentheses are right there. Yet Python insists `sizes` is a plain number. What turns a value into a tuple in Python, if it is not the parentheses she was relying on?","body":["A tuple is an ordered sequence like a list, but immutable: once created, its items cannot be added, removed or replaced. You usually write a tuple with parentheses and commas, `point = (3, 4)`, but it is actually the comma that makes a tuple. `t = 1, 2, 3` (tuple packing) creates a tuple without parentheses. An empty tuple is `()` or `tuple()`, and `tuple(\"ab\")` gives `('a', 'b')`, just as `tuple([1, 2])` gives `(1, 2)`. Tuples exist because many values naturally belong together and should not change: a coordinate, a date, an RGB (red, green, blue) color."],"locked":true},{"t":"Dictionaries: building, indexing, adding, changing and removing keys","hook":"Nora handles the help desk queue at Silver Lake Credit Union, and a script that counts failed login attempts per username just crashed in the middle of a busy Monday with \"KeyError: 'jlee'\". The counter worked for weeks, because every user it saw had failed before. Today a new employee mistyped a password for the first time, and the line `fails[user] += 1` gave up. The security team needs those counts to spot suspicious activity. Why does adding one to a count fail only for someone who has never been counted, and what one-line change makes the script safe?","body":["A dictionary (type `dict`) stores key-value pairs. Instead of looking items up by numeric position, as with a list, you look them up by a meaningful key, the way you look up a word in a real dictionary to find its definition. You write a dictionary in curly braces with a colon between each key and its value and commas between pairs: `ages = {\"Ana\": 31, \"Ben\": 27}`. An empty dictionary is `{}` or `dict()`. Note that `{}` is an empty dict, not an empty set. You can also build one from pairs, as in `dict([(\"a\", 1), (\"b\", 2)])`, or with keyword arguments, as in `dict(a=1, b=2)`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Iterating dictionaries with keys(), values() and items(); checking whether a key exists","hook":"Marcus runs the nightly stock report for Cedar Street Pharmacy. His script holds a dictionary of medicine names and quantities, and a rule says to flag anything with a count of 0. He writes `if 0 in stock:` and the report says nothing is out of stock, even though he can see three items at zero. Worse, when he tries to tidy the dictionary by deleting zero-count items inside his loop, the script stops with \"RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration.\" The pharmacist needs the reorder list before the morning delivery cutoff. What is `in` really checking, and how should he loop when he wants to remove entries?","body":["Once data is in a dictionary, you will often need to walk through all of it: print a report, total the values, or find the largest entry. Dictionaries give you three views of their contents. `d.keys()` returns the keys, `d.values()` returns the values, and `d.items()` returns the key-value pairs as two-item tuples. These are view objects, not lists: they reflect later changes to the dictionary, and you can loop over them, test membership in them or convert them with `list()`. Printing one shows something like `dict_keys(['a', 'b'])`, and views cannot be indexed, so `d.keys()[0]` raises `TypeError`; use `list(d.keys())[0]` if you really need a position."],"locked":true},{"t":"Strings: indexing, slicing (including [::-1]), immutability and comparison","hook":"Grace maintains the catalog for the Brookside Public Library, and patrons keep reporting the same oddities. A search sorted by title places \"Zen Garden\" before \"art of war,\" and \"Volume 10\" appears before \"Volume 9.\" Then a new feature to capitalize the first letter of each author's name fails with \"TypeError: 'str' object does not support item assignment.\" The head librarian wants a fix and an explanation by the end of the day. All three problems trace back to how Python stores and compares text. What rules is Python following that make these results correct by its own logic?","body":["A string is an immutable sequence of characters. Because it is a sequence, it supports the same indexing and slicing rules as lists and tuples, so everything you learned there carries over. `s = \"Python\"` has length 6; `s[0]` is `'P'`, `s[5]` and `s[-1]` are both `'n'`, and `s[6]` raises `IndexError: string index out of range`. Python has no separate character type: indexing a string returns another string of length one. You can loop over a string character by character with `for ch in s:`, and `in` tests for a substring, so `\"th\" in s` is `True`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Escaping with \\, quotes and apostrophes inside strings, multi-line strings","hook":"Omar is on the IT team at Westbrook Clinic, and the nightly backup script just produced a report that makes no sense. The line meant to read `Saved to C:\\backups\\new` instead prints `Saved to C:\\backups` and then, on a new line, `ew`. A coworker's quick fix to add a quoted file name made things worse: the script now refuses to run at all, with a SyntaxError pointing at a double quote. The compliance officer reads these reports every morning. Nothing about the backup itself is broken. So why is Python printing something different from what Omar typed?","body":["A string literal is text in your source code delimited by quotes, so putting quote characters inside it needs care: Python has to know which quote ends the string. Python gives you two easy ways out. First, choose the other kind of quote for the delimiters: `\"It's fine\"` contains an apostrophe inside double quotes, and `'She said \"hi\"'` contains double quotes inside single quotes. Single and double quotes are otherwise identical in Python; neither is \"more correct\". Second, escape the quote with a backslash: `'It\\'s fine'` and `\"She said \\\"hi\\\"\"` are both valid and produce the same text."],"locked":true},{"t":"Common string methods: split(), join(), upper(), lower(), strip(), find(), count(), replace()","hook":"Hannah handles the newsletter sign-ups for the Oak Valley Garden Club. Members type their names and emails into a web form, and the export arrives as messy lines like \"  Ana MARTINEZ ; ana@Example.com \" with stray spaces and odd capitals. Her cleanup script runs without errors, yet the printed list looks exactly as messy as the input. She checks the code and sees lines such as `name.strip()` and `email.lower()`, each on its own. The club president wants clean mailing labels by tomorrow. If the methods ran, where did their results go?","body":["Strings have many built-in methods, called with dot notation such as `text.upper()`. They are the everyday tools for cleaning user input, parsing lines of data and building output. Because strings are immutable, none of these methods change the original string. Each returns a new value, and you must assign or use that result. Writing `name.strip()` on its own line and expecting `name` to change is a classic bug; the correct form is `name = name.strip()`."],"locked":true},{"t":"Decomposition: splitting a program into functions","hook":"Ethan inherited the order-processing script at Bayside Bakery Supply, all 300 lines of it in one long block. When the sales tax rate changes, he updates the calculation where he finds it, runs a test order, and everything looks fine. A week later, the accountant reports that some invoices still use the old rate. The tax formula had been copied into three different places, and Ethan found only two. Nobody made a typo, and the code runs without errors. What structural change would have made this mistake nearly impossible?","body":["As programs grow, putting all the code in one long sequence becomes hard to read, test and change. Decomposition is the practice of breaking a problem into smaller, well-defined sub-problems and giving each one its own function. Instead of one 200-line script, you might have `read_scores()`, `average()`, `grade_for()` and `print_report()`, and a short main section that calls them in order. Reading the main section then tells you the whole story of the program in a few lines, and each detail lives in one clearly named place."],"locked":true},{"t":"Defining and invoking functions; functions must be defined before they are called","hook":"Rosa is building a quiz app for the Elm Grove Adult Learning Center, and the first test run ends immediately with \"NameError: name 'ask_question' is not defined.\" The function is right there in the file, a few lines down, spelled exactly the same. After she moves things around, the app runs but prints `<function score at 0x...>` where a number should be, and a fix she made to `score()` seems to be ignored. The center's instructors want to try the app this afternoon. What does Python actually do, line by line, when it meets a `def` and when it meets a call?","body":["You define a function with the `def` keyword, followed by the function name, a pair of parentheses containing any parameters, and a colon. The indented block below is the function body, and it must contain at least one statement (`pass` will do). Defining a function does not run the body; it creates a function object and binds it to the name. The body runs only when you call (invoke) the function by writing its name followed by parentheses, with any arguments inside. Each call runs the body from the top, with fresh local variables."],"locked":true},{"t":"Return and yield, returning several values as a tuple, the None value","hook":"It is your second week supporting the volunteer-built reservation system at Cedar Hollow Public Library. A librarian, Priya, forwards a screenshot: every confirmation email now reads \"Your pickup shelf is None.\" The function that finds the shelf clearly works, because when you run it by hand it prints \"Shelf B4\" right there on the screen. Yet the email code, which calls the same function, gets nothing useful back. Nobody changed the email template and nobody touched the database. The only recent edit was a \"small cleanup\" that swapped one word in the shelf function. Which word turns a working answer into None, and how do you get the answer back?","body":["Functions are only truly useful when they can hand results back, and the PCEP (Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer) exam tests exactly how that hand-off works. The `return` statement ends a function immediately and sends a value back to the caller. The call expression then evaluates to that value, so you can assign it, print it or use it in a larger expression: `total = add(2, 3)` or `print(add(2, 3) * 10)`. Any code after an executed `return` in the same function is skipped, and even a `return` inside a loop ends the whole function, not just the loop. A function can contain several `return` statements, for example one in each branch of an `if`, but only one of them runs per call."],"locked":true},{"t":"Recursion, base cases and RecursionError","hook":"Tomas, the only developer at Larkspur Community Theater, wrote a tiny function to count every seat in the venue's nested seating map: sections contain rows, rows contain blocks, and blocks contain seats. It worked on the test map. Tonight the box office manager uploads the new balcony layout, and the ticketing script dies with a wall of identical traceback lines ending in \"maximum recursion depth exceeded\". Doors open in two hours. The function calls itself, which is the whole idea, so why does it suddenly never stop, and which single line decides whether recursion finishes neatly or runs away?","body":["A recursive function is one that calls itself. Recursion is a way of solving a problem by reducing it to a smaller version of the same problem, solving that, and building the answer back up. The factorial of n (the product of all integers from 1 to n, written in math with an exclamation mark after the number) is the standard example: the factorial of n equals n times the factorial of n - 1, and the factorial of 1 is 1. The definition refers to itself, so the code can too. Recursion is not a special Python feature; it simply follows from the fact that a function body may call any function that exists when it runs, including itself."],"locked":true},{"t":"Parameters vs arguments; positional, keyword and mixed argument passing","hook":"At Willow Bend Veterinary Clinic, the appointment script has worked for months. This morning Dana at the front desk calls you: a golden retriever named Biscuit was supposed to be booked for 45 minutes in exam room 3, but the calendar shows a 3-minute visit in room 45, a room that does not exist. You open the code and find the call a new volunteer added. It passes the same three values the function always expects, just typed in a different order than the definition lists them. Python raised no error and printed no warning; it did exactly what it was told. How does Python decide which value goes where, and how could the call have been written so the mix-up was impossible?","body":["The words parameter and argument are often used loosely, but the exam uses them precisely. A parameter is the name listed in the function definition; it exists only inside the function. An argument is the actual value you pass when calling the function. In `def area(width, height):`, `width` and `height` are parameters; in `area(3, 4)`, 3 and 4 are arguments. When the function is called, each parameter is bound to its argument, and the parameter then behaves like a local variable for the duration of that call. A handy memory aid: parameters are placeholders in the definition, arguments are actual values in the call."],"locked":true},{"t":"Default parameter values and why defaults must follow required parameters","hook":"Felix maintains the label-printing script at Juniper Ridge Food Bank. Every Saturday, volunteers print bin labels with `make_label(item)`, and it has always worked. Yesterday he added an optional size setting and, while he was at it, a new required parameter for the storage zone, typed at the end of the parameter list. This morning the volunteers report that the script will not even start: no labels, just a SyntaxError pointing at the def line, before a single function has been called. Felix only added one word. Why does Python refuse to create the function at all, and where should that word have gone?","body":["A parameter can be given a default value in the function definition with `name=value`. If the caller supplies an argument for it, that argument is used; if not, the default is used. This lets one function serve both simple and detailed calls, and it lets you add new options to an existing function without breaking the calls that already use it. `print()` itself works this way: `sep` defaults to a space and `end` to a newline, which is why `print(\"a\", \"b\")` shows `a b` and moves to a new line unless you say otherwise."],"locked":true},{"t":"Name scopes, shadowing and the global keyword; UnboundLocalError","hook":"The scoreboard for the Brookfield Middle School robotics fair runs on a short Python script written by a parent volunteer, Ines. At 10 a.m. the first team scores, and the projector shows a traceback instead of points: UnboundLocalError. Ines is puzzled, because the variable `score` is right there at the top of the file, set to zero, and another function reads it without any trouble. In a hurry she changes one line so the error goes away, and now the board shows zero for every team, all morning, without a single complaint. Why can one function see `score` while another cannot, and why is the quiet version worse?","body":["A name's scope is the part of the program where that name can be seen and used. Names assigned at the top level of a script live in the global scope and are visible everywhere in the file, including inside functions. Names created inside a function, including its parameters, are local: they exist only while that call is running and cannot be seen from outside. Using a function's local variable after the function returns raises `NameError`. Scopes keep functions independent: a variable called `total` inside one function never collides with a `total` inside another."],"locked":true},{"t":"The exception hierarchy: BaseException, Exception, SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt, ArithmeticError, LookupError","hook":"It is late Friday at Saltmarsh Wind Co-op, and Jonah is running an overnight script that cleans six months of turbine readings. Partway through, he spots a wrong file path and presses Ctrl+C. Nothing stops. The script prints \"skipped one bad record\" and carries on. He presses again: another skipped record. Eventually he has to close the terminal window, losing the run's log. The handler that swallowed his interrupt was meant to catch only bad data. Why was Ctrl+C treated like a bad record, and which single word in that handler would have let him stop the script cleanly?","body":["When something goes wrong at run time, Python raises an exception: an object describing the error, with a type (its class) and usually a message. Exceptions are organized into a class hierarchy, a family tree in which more specific exception types inherit from more general ones. This matters because an `except` clause that names a class also catches every class below it in the tree. Knowing the tree lets you predict which handler runs, and lets you choose how broad or narrow each handler should be."],"locked":true},{"t":"Common built-in exceptions: ZeroDivisionError, IndexError, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError, NameError","hook":"Monday morning at Pinecrest Adult Learning Center, Rosa opens the help-desk queue and finds five tickets about the same homework-grading tool. Each ticket includes a screenshot of a traceback, and each ends with a different last line: ValueError, ZeroDivisionError, KeyError, TypeError and NameError. The instructor who filed them believes the tool is simply broken and wants it rewritten from scratch. Rosa suspects something else: five small, very specific problems, each one announced clearly by Python itself. What is each of those names telling her, and which two are people most likely to mix up?","body":["The PCEP (Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer) exam expects you to look at a short snippet and name the exception it raises. Six built-in exceptions come up again and again. Learn what triggers each one and how they differ, especially the pair `TypeError` and `ValueError`, which accounts for many wrong answers. When an exception is not handled, Python prints a traceback ending with a line like `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero`; the last line names the exception type and message, which is where to look first."],"locked":true},{"t":"Try-except, except with several exceptions, bare except and except Exception","hook":"Kofi volunteers at the Riverside Tenants' Association, where a small Python script records monthly rent-relief applications typed in at the front desk. Last month it crashed every time someone typed a dollar sign in the income field, so a well-meaning helper wrapped everything in a bare `except:`. Now it never crashes. It also never stops: Ctrl+C does nothing. And when an application vanished last week, there was no error anywhere, because a misspelled variable was being quietly treated as bad input. The script looks calmer and is far more dangerous. How do you catch the errors you expect without hiding the ones you do not?","body":["Exception handling lets your program respond to errors instead of crashing. You put code that might fail in a `try` block and the recovery code in one or more `except` blocks. If no exception occurs in the `try` block, all `except` blocks are skipped. If an exception occurs, the rest of the `try` block is abandoned immediately, and Python looks for the first `except` clause whose type matches the exception or one of its parent classes. If one matches, its block runs and execution continues after the whole statement. If none matches, the exception continues upward as if there were no handler."],"locked":true},{"t":"Ordering except branches from specific to general","hook":"At Northgate Transit's small IT office, riders have been complaining that the trip-planner tool says \"Something went wrong\" for every problem, whether they typed a stop name that does not exist or a departure time like 25:99. The developer, Lena, insists she wrote helpful messages for each case. You open her handler code and find them all there, carefully worded: \"Unknown stop\", \"Invalid time\", and a general fallback. Yet none of the specific messages has appeared once in three months of logs, and Python never complained about anything. How can correctly written handlers sit in the code and never run?","body":["When an exception is raised inside a `try` block, Python checks the `except` clauses from top to bottom and runs the first one that matches. A clause matches if the exception is an instance of the named class or of any of its subclasses. Once a clause has matched, no later clauses are considered, even if a later one names the exact class that was raised. This first-match rule is why the order of `except` branches matters, and why PCEP (Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer) questions so often show several branches and ask which one prints. Consider what happens if a general class comes first. `except Exception:` matches almost every error, and `except LookupError:` matches both `IndexError` and `KeyError`. If you put one of these above a more specific clause, the specific clause can never run: it is unreachable, or dead code. Python does not report an error for this; the code runs normally, and the specific branch simply never gets its turn."],"locked":true},{"t":"Propagating exceptions through function boundaries and deciding where to handle them","hook":"Amara is on call for the online booking system at Heronsgate Community Pool. A parent emails: the site accepted her child's swim-lesson booking, showed a cheerful \"Booked for None\", and reserved no lane. You trace the flow. The booking page calls `create_booking()`, which calls `pick_slot()`, which calls a helper that parses the lesson time. That helper has a tidy `try` and `except` that catches every bad time and quietly returns None. Nothing crashed, nothing was logged, and three layers up, the page had no idea anything had failed. Was catching the error there the right call, and if not, where should it have been caught?","body":["An exception does not have to be handled in the function where it is raised. If a function has no matching `except` for an exception, the function stops immediately, and the exception is passed (propagated) to the code that called it, at the point of the call. If that caller does not handle it either, it moves up again, through each calling function in turn. If it reaches the top level of the program without being handled, Python prints a traceback and the program ends. This behavior is what lets you separate the code that detects a problem from the code that decides what to do about it."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-10-07"});
